<!-- doc/src/sgml/otel.sgml -->

<sect1 id="otel" xreflabel="otel">
 <title>otel &mdash; OpenTelemetry trace-context infrastructure</title>

 <indexterm zone="otel">
  <primary>otel</primary>
 </indexterm>

 <para>
  The <filename>otel</filename> module is the API/infrastructure
  half of <productname>PostgreSQL</productname>'s built-in
  OpenTelemetry trace-context support.  It implements the
  client-side context-ingestion mechanisms (the <literal>'M'</literal>
  per-message protocol header, the <varname>otel.traceparent</varname>
  and <varname>otel.tracestate</varname> GUCs, optional
  <ulink url="https://google.github.io/sqlcommenter/">sqlcommenter</ulink>
  parsing), a sampler-hook framework, an active-span stack for
  producer-side instrumentation, parallel-worker context propagation
  via shared memory, and a rendezvous-published
  <structname>OtelTracingApi</structname> that out-of-tree exporters
  and other contrib modules consume.
 </para>

 <para>
  By itself <filename>otel</filename> does not produce any spans for
  ordinary SQL queries.  Statement-level tracing is the responsibility
  of a companion module such as the in-tree
  <xref linkend="otel-postgres-tracing"/>, which builds on the
  <filename>otel</filename> API.  An installation that loads only
  <filename>otel</filename> has the protocol-header mechanism and the
  log-side trace-correlation fields available, but no query spans.
 </para>

 <sect2 id="otel-loading">
  <title>Loading the module</title>

  <para>
   <filename>otel</filename> must be loaded via
   <xref linkend="guc-shared-preload-libraries"/> because it installs
   protocol-header handlers, registers rendezvous variables, and
   allocates shared memory at postmaster start.  A
   <command>CREATE EXTENSION</command> entry is also provided for
   catalogue tracking and for the introspection function it exposes
   (see <xref linkend="otel-sql-surface"/>).
  </para>

  <para>
   To get query-side tracing as well, load
   <filename>otel_postgres_tracing</filename> immediately after.  The
   order matters: <filename>otel</filename> publishes the
   <structname>OtelTracingApi</structname> rendezvous variable at its
   <function>_PG_init</function>, and
   <filename>otel_postgres_tracing</filename> looks it up at its own
   <function>_PG_init</function>.
  </para>

<programlisting>
# postgresql.conf
shared_preload_libraries = 'otel,otel_postgres_tracing'
</programlisting>
 </sect2>

 <sect2 id="otel-configuration-parameters">
  <title>Configuration Parameters</title>

  <variablelist>
   <varlistentry>
    <term>
     <varname>otel.traceparent</varname> (<type>text</type>)
     <indexterm>
      <primary><varname>otel.traceparent</varname> configuration parameter</primary>
     </indexterm>
    </term>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      Client-supplied W3C TraceContext
      <literal>traceparent</literal> value, e.g.
      <literal>00-{32-hex-trace-id}-{16-hex-span-id}-{2-hex-flags}</literal>.
      Normally set via the per-message <literal>'M'</literal>
      RequestHeaders protocol mechanism; can also be set with
      <command>SET</command> / <command>SET LOCAL</command> for
      clients that do not speak the <literal>'M'</literal> message.
      Empty by default.
     </para>
    </listitem>
   </varlistentry>

   <varlistentry>
    <term>
     <varname>otel.tracestate</varname> (<type>text</type>)
     <indexterm>
      <primary><varname>otel.tracestate</varname> configuration parameter</primary>
     </indexterm>
    </term>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      Optional W3C TraceContext <literal>tracestate</literal> value
      (vendor-specific key=value entries).  Companion to
      <varname>otel.traceparent</varname>.
     </para>
    </listitem>
   </varlistentry>

   <varlistentry>
    <term>
     <varname>otel.emit_spans_to_log</varname> (<type>boolean</type>)
     <indexterm>
      <primary><varname>otel.emit_spans_to_log</varname> configuration parameter</primary>
     </indexterm>
    </term>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      When on, every emitted span is also written to the postgres log
      as a JSON line prefixed with <literal>otel-span:</literal>.
      Off by default.  Useful when no exporter module is loaded, or
      for debugging the in-process span flow without setting up an
      external collector.  Reload-only.
     </para>
    </listitem>
   </varlistentry>

   <varlistentry>
    <term>
     <varname>otel.parse_sqlcommenter</varname> (<type>boolean</type>)
     <indexterm>
      <primary><varname>otel.parse_sqlcommenter</varname> configuration parameter</primary>
     </indexterm>
    </term>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      When on, trace context is extracted from
      <ulink url="https://google.github.io/sqlcommenter/">sqlcommenter</ulink>-style
      SQL comments in the query text, used as a fallback when no
      <literal>'M'</literal> header or
      <varname>otel.traceparent</varname> GUC has been set.  Off by
      default.
     </para>
     <warning>
      <para>
       Sqlcommenter is structurally broken under client-side or
       server-side prepared statements: the comment is captured at
       Parse time and frozen in the cached plan, so every subsequent
       Execute reuses the original trace context.  Prefer the
       <literal>'M'</literal> protocol header for any workload that
       uses prepared statements.
      </para>
     </warning>
    </listitem>
   </varlistentry>
  </variablelist>
 </sect2>

 <sect2 id="otel-sql-surface">
  <title>SQL surface</title>

  <variablelist>
   <varlistentry>
    <term>
     <function>otel_current_traceparent()</function> returns <type>text</type>
     <indexterm>
      <primary><function>otel_current_traceparent</function></primary>
     </indexterm>
    </term>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      Returns the currently-active <literal>traceparent</literal>
      reconstructed from the in-memory root context, or
      <literal>NULL</literal> if no context is set.
     </para>
    </listitem>
   </varlistentry>
  </variablelist>
 </sect2>

 <sect2 id="otel-headers-message">
  <title>The <literal>'M'</literal> RequestHeaders protocol message</title>

  <para>
   The primary client &rarr; server propagation path is the
   <literal>'M'</literal> RequestHeaders message added to the v3
   protocol by this module (negotiated via the
   <literal>_pq_.headers=1</literal> startup option).  A client sends
   namespaced <literal>(key, value)</literal> pairs in an
   <literal>'M'</literal> message; the
   <literal>_otel.traceparent</literal> and
   <literal>_otel.tracestate</literal> keys populate the backend's
   root context for the next statement.
  </para>

  <para>
   <filename>otel</filename> affirmatively acknowledges support via a
   <literal>protocol_features</literal> ParameterStatus message at
   connection startup.  Proxies (notably pgbouncer) that strip the
   <literal>_pq_.headers=1</literal> opt-in are therefore observable
   to the client: no acknowledgement arrives, and the libpq
   <function>PQheadersAvailable()</function> API returns false.
   See <xref linkend="libpq-headers"/> for the client-side details.
  </para>
 </sect2>

 <sect2 id="otel-rendezvous-api">
  <title>The <structname>OtelTracingApi</structname> rendezvous interface</title>

  <para>
   Out-of-tree exporter modules and other contrib extensions consume
   <filename>otel</filename>'s functionality via a function-pointer
   table published as a postgres rendezvous variable named
   <literal>OtelTracingApi</literal>.  Consumers locate it with
   <function>find_rendezvous_variable()</function> at
   <function>_PG_init</function> time, verify the major/minor
   version, and call into the table for hook registration
   (exporters), producer-side span construction (instrumentation),
   active-stack inspection (cross-module nesting), parallel-worker
   propagation, sqlcommenter parsing, and sampler dispatch.
  </para>

  <para>
   The full interface declaration is in
   <filename>contrib/otel/otel_api.h</filename>; the related data
   types in <filename>contrib/otel/otel.h</filename>.  Both headers
   are installed under
   <filename>$(pg_config&nbsp;--includedir-server)/extension/otel/</filename>
   so consumer modules can <literal>#include &lt;otel/otel.h&gt;</literal>.
  </para>

  <para>
   Versioning follows a major/minor halfword convention encoded in a
   single <type>uint32</type>: the high halfword is bumped on any
   incompatible layout change (strict-equality check, force rebuild);
   the low halfword is a monotonic extension counter (additive-only,
   consumer requires <literal>producer_minor &gt;= consumer_minor</literal>).
   See the comment on
   <literal>OTEL_TRACING_API_VERSION</literal> in
   <filename>otel_api.h</filename>.
  </para>
 </sect2>

 <sect2 id="otel-parallel-workers">
  <title>Parallel-worker context propagation</title>

  <para>
   When a query that has an active span launches parallel workers,
   workers attribute their own spans to the leader's currently-active
   span_id (not the client-supplied root span_id).  This is
   implemented via a fixed-size shared-memory slot array indexed by
   <literal>ProcNumber</literal>: the leader publishes its
   SpanContext to its own slot at span start; workers read their
   leader's slot via <varname>ParallelLeaderProcNumber</varname>.
   The mechanism is not exposed through GUCs; it is internal to the
   <structname>OtelTracingApi</structname>.
  </para>
 </sect2>

 <sect2 id="otel-log-correlation">
  <title>Log/trace correlation</title>

  <para>
   When a backend has an active trace context (either client-supplied
   or via the in-process active-span stack), core
   <productname>PostgreSQL</productname>'s JSON and CSV log writers
   include <literal>trace_id</literal>, <literal>span_id</literal>,
   and <literal>trace_flags</literal> as top-level fields, and the
   <literal>%T</literal> / <literal>%S</literal>
   <xref linkend="guc-log-line-prefix"/> escapes substitute them into
   plain-text log lines.  This is implemented in the core elog/ereport
   machinery; <filename>otel</filename> populates the
   <structname>ErrorData</structname> fields via an
   <varname>emit_log_hook</varname>-style integration when a span is
   active.
  </para>
 </sect2>
</sect1>
